(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cutaneous-bacterial-infections-quiz"]={"title":"Cutaneous Bacterial Infections Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What level of the skin does impetigo infect, and which organisms cause it?","choices":["An infection of the upper dermis and superficial lymphatics, most often Streptococcus pyogenes","An infection of the deeper dermis and subcutaneous tissue, streptococcal or staphylococcal","An infection of the tissue surrounding muscle and fascia, usually polymicrobial in nature","A superficial epidermal infection, most often Staphylococcus aureus or Streptococcus pyogenes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is very contagious and autoinoculable, and common in infants and children. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"How does acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis present after impetigo?","choices":["Gradual weight loss, painless jaundice, pale stools and dark urine over several weeks","Fever with a sandpaper rash, strawberry tongue and circumoral pallor within days","Migratory joint pain, a new heart murmur and subcutaneous nodules over the elbows","Sudden oedema, haematuria with smoky or tea-coloured urine, proteinuria and hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, especially in three to seven year olds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"What is a furuncle?","choices":["A deep-seated abscess of a hair follicle and the adjacent subcutaneous tissue","An inflammation of the follicle wall and ostia forming a superficial pustule","A collection of purulent material seeded by traumatic inoculation of bacteria","An infection of the upper dermis extending into superficial skin lymphatics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Staphylococcus aureus is the commonest organism. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which topical agent is an alternative comedolytic for patients who cannot tolerate a retinoid?","choices":["Dapsone gel, an anti-inflammatory with an unknown mechanism","Salicylic acid, a beta-hydroxy acid that is oil-soluble","Benzoyl peroxide, a topical antimicrobial and keratolytic","Clindamycin, a topical antimicrobial active against the follicle"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It softens keratin and dissolves the debris that clogs pores. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Why may an area of necrotizing fasciitis stop being tender as the disease advances?","choices":["The antibiotic has reached therapeutic levels","The superficial nerves have been destroyed","The inflammatory response has begun to resolve","The infection has been contained by the fascia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, alongside skin colour change to blue-grey, bullae and cutaneous gangrene. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 127"},{"q":"What isolation advice is given for a child with impetigo?","choices":["Isolate until every crusted lesion has completely healed and the skin is clear","No isolation is needed at any point, since impetigo does not spread person to person","Isolate for a full ten days from the appearance of the very first crusted lesion","Isolate until treatment has been underway for twenty-four to forty-eight hours"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and do not share towels, clothing, bath water, washcloths or razors. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"Where does erythrasma most commonly appear?","choices":["Inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes","Back of the neck, face, axillae and buttocks, favouring areas of friction","Face and extremities, spreading by scratching and self-inoculation","Lower extremity in most patients, almost never affecting both legs"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Less common sites are the axilla, under the breasts and intergluteal folds. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"How does chronic paronychia present, and over what timescale?","choices":["Grouped painful vesicles on the distal finger appearing over twenty-four hours","Oedematous, erythematous, tender nail folds without fluctuance, present at least six weeks","Rapid onset of erythema and swelling two to five days after trauma, often fluctuant","Painless thickening and yellowing of the nail plate over months without inflammation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nail plates thicken and discolour, and cuticles separate from the plate. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"How does purulence change the antibiotic choice in cellulitis?","choices":["Purulent cellulitis prompts topical mupirocin in addition to the oral antibiotic","Purulent cellulitis prompts oral fluconazole because yeast is likely to be present","Purulent cellulitis prompts cover for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus","Purulent cellulitis prompts a switch to penicillin V to cover Group A streptococcus"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole, doxycycline, clindamycin or linezolid. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotic is described as the drug of choice for impetigo in children?","choices":["Dicloxacillin","Erythromycin","Cephalexin","Doxycycline"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Clindamycin is used where there is penicillin allergy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 95"},{"q":"What does the 2016 American Academy of Dermatology guideline recommend for comedonal acne?","choices":["A topical retinoid with an oral antibiotic and topical benzoyl peroxide together","Oral isotretinoin as monotherapy over a sixteen to twenty week treatment course","A topical retinoid, switching to azelaic or salicylic acid if it is not tolerated","A topical antimicrobial with a topical retinoid, or benzoyl peroxide with an antibiotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the entry point of the treatment ladder. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Which risk factors are named for necrotizing fasciitis?","choices":["Manicure, hangnail, ingrown nail and the habit of biting or picking at the nails","Trauma, burns, surgery, immunosuppression, renal failure, alcoholism, dental infection and injection drug use","Obesity, poor hygiene, occlusive clothing, hot humid weather and nasal staphylococcal carriage","Mastectomy, tinea pedis, obesity and any pre-existing superficial skin infection"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and it occurs more often in males than females. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 125"},{"q":"Which three elements are required to diagnose hidradenitis suppurativa?","choices":["Coral-red fluorescence under a Wood's lamp, intertriginous distribution, and a history of diabetes mellitus","Typical lesions, characteristic distribution in axilla and groin, and recurrence more than twice in six months","Positive bacterial culture, characteristic distribution over the trunk, and failure of oral antibiotic therapy","A biopsy showing apocrine gland destruction, raised inflammatory markers, and a family history of disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It is primarily a clinical diagnosis; biopsy is not usually required. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What is the typical timing and distribution of hot tub folliculitis?","choices":["Onset within forty-eight hours, on the lower extremity with a demarcated border","Onset three to seven days after a sore throat, on the face and extremities","Onset eight hours to five days after exposure, on the trunk, extremities and buttocks","Onset two to five days after trauma, around the fingernail folds of the hand"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It usually spares the face, neck, soles and palms. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"How is a cutaneous abscess treated?","choices":["Warm compresses alone in every case, since abscesses reliably resolve without any intervention","Wide surgical excision of the affected area, since recurrence is otherwise close to universal","Surgical incision and drainage if it does not drain spontaneously; warm soaks and antibiotics if it does","Oral antibiotics alone in every case, since incision risks spreading the infection into the blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum cover considers methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, then narrows to culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"How is acne vulgaris diagnosed?","choices":["By bacterial culture of a pustule taken before treatment begins","By punch biopsy of an inflammatory lesion sent for histology","By measuring serum androgens in every patient at presentation","Clinically, with culture reserved for cases that fail to respond"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. No routine test is needed to make the diagnosis. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What should be suspected when acne scarring seems out of proportion to the severity of lesions?","choices":["Patient manipulation of the lesions through picking or squeezing","An undiagnosed hyperandrogenic state driving deeper inflammation","Resistance of Cutibacterium acnes to the prescribed antimicrobial","An underlying immunodeficiency impairing the healing of each lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients and families are educated about that risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the expected course of cellulitis on antibiotic therapy?","choices":["It may look worse on day one, fever usually resolves within twenty-four hours, inflammation over one to two weeks","It improves visibly within hours, fever resolves within six hours, and erythema clears within three days","It worsens for the first week before any improvement, with fever expected to persist throughout","It resolves without any change in appearance, since the erythema is not caused by the infection"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fever beyond forty-eight hours prompts a change guided by culture. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"When should cultures be obtained in impetigo?","choices":["When the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus, or when post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is present","In every case at first presentation, since staphylococcal and streptococcal disease are treated differently from one another","Only when the lesions have failed to respond after a full two-week course of topical mupirocin","Only in children under three years of age, since they are the group at highest risk of complications"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a health-care worker or teacher is the example given of high risk. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"Which oral antibiotics are most commonly used for acne, and what is used when they are contraindicated?","choices":["Doxycycline and minocycline, with erythromycin when a tetracycline is contraindicated","Cephalexin and dicloxacillin, with clindamycin when a penicillin is contraindicated","Erythromycin and clarithromycin, with miconazole when a macrolide is contraindicated","Penicillin V and amoxicillin, with azithromycin when a penicillin is contraindicated"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Sarecycline, a tetracycline derivative, was approved in 2018 for moderate to severe acne. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What is acute paronychia and what predisposes to it?","choices":["Viral infection of the distal finger producing grouped vesicles on an erythematous base","Infection of the soft tissue around the fingernail, from manicure, ingrown nail, hangnail or nail biting","Inflammation of the proximal nail fold from irritants and allergens, in cleaners, cooks and bartenders","Infection of the fingertip pulp space producing throbbing pain and a tense, swollen finger pad"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. It starts as cellulitis and progresses to abscess; Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus pyogenes are typical. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"Which portals of entry are named for cellulitis?","choices":["Manicure, ingrown nail, hangnail and nail biting affecting the soft tissue of the finger","Contaminated whirlpools, hot tubs, water slides and inadequately chlorinated pools","Tinea pedis, open lesion, trauma, surgical wound, insect bite, fissure and radiation therapy","Nasal carriage of Staphylococcus aureus, shaving, occlusive clothing and hot humid weather"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Those are distinct from the risk factors, which include diabetes and lymphoedema. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"How does erysipelas present systemically?","choices":["Sudden onset of malaise, myalgias, chills and high fever within forty-eight hours of skin changes","Gradual onset of low-grade fever and fatigue over the two weeks before the rash appears","No systemic symptoms at all, with the patient consistently afebrile and otherwise well","Unrelenting pain out of proportion to examination, with skin colour changing to blue-grey"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with nausea, headache, pruritus, burning, tenderness and swelling. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"How is chronic paronychia treated?","choices":["Warm soaks three times daily with incision and drainage if purulent material has collected","Oral cephalexin for ten days with removal of the nail plate if there is no early response","Topical mupirocin applied to the nail fold three times daily until the swelling settles","Keep the hands dry and use a broad-spectrum topical antifungal, with oral fluconazole in severe cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — treat the underlying inflammation and infection together. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"What defines folliculitis?","choices":["Inflammatory cells within the wall and ostia of the hair follicle, forming a follicular pustule","A deep-seated abscess of the hair follicle and the adjacent subcutaneous tissue","Inflammation of the cutaneous apocrine glands of the axilla, groin or perineum","A foreign body reaction to a cut hair that curves back into the wall of the follicle"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The causes include infection, physical injury and chemical irritation. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"When are oral antibiotics indicated in acne vulgaris?","choices":["For patients with acne scarring, in order to prevent any further scars from developing over time","For moderate to severe acne too widespread for topicals, or after an inadequate response over six to eight weeks","For comedonal acne at first presentation, before any topical therapy has been trialled at all","For any patient whose acne has failed to clear completely within two weeks of starting treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and they are prescribed for the shortest time possible, about three to four months. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What does the 2016 guideline recommend for severe nodular acne?","choices":["A topical retinoid alone, switching to azelaic or salicylic acid if it is not tolerated","Benzoyl peroxide with a topical antibiotic and no retinoid at any stage of treatment","An oral contraceptive pill with spironolactone as the initial approach in all patients","A topical retinoid with an oral antibiotic and benzoyl peroxide, or isotretinoin monotherapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Moderate disease gets the same triple combination without the isotretinoin alternative. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which measure is recommended when methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus is a concern in a drained furuncle?","choices":["Start linezolid empirically before any material is obtained","Swab the nasal vestibule of every household contact first","Defer drainage until the culture result becomes available","Culture the material obtained at incision and drainage"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and give endocarditis prophylaxis to at-risk patients. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which shaving advice is given for pseudofolliculitis barbae?","choices":["Continue the current routine and treat each papule with a topical antibiotic","Stop shaving if possible, use clean razors, and avoid lift-and-cut razor systems","Shave daily against the grain with a multi-blade razor to cut hairs below the surface","Shave weekly with an electric razor set to the closest possible cutting length"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — mild razor angles and single or at most double blades are preferred. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which single clue is emphasised for recognising necrotizing fasciitis early?","choices":["Honey-coloured crusting over a superficial epidermal erosion","Coral-red fluorescence of the affected skin under a Wood's lamp","Unrelenting pain out of proportion to the physical examination","A sharply demarcated plaque raised above the surrounding skin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — patients are often diagnosed with cellulitis, sent home, and return worse. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 126"}]};
